WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.440
”Star Wars" fans argue so much about the successes&nbsp;
of the franchise, it's easy to let the true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:12.720
failures slip your mind. The Kinect game? The&nbsp;
3D re-releases? "Detours"? Those and more ahead.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.200
If you thought Star Wars didn't enter the world of&nbsp;
animated television until the 21st century, you'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.640
be wrong — but you wouldn't be alone, either.&nbsp;
After all, Lucasfilm has largely buried the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.800
company's first foray into this particular genre,&nbsp;
one that occurred as far back as the 1980s. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.160
when Star Wars: Droids attempted to make waves&nbsp;
in the world of Saturday morning programming.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.360
However, the show struggled&nbsp;
for a number of reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.680
For one, it had a pretty hefty price tag,&nbsp;
with each episode running between $500,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.640
and $600,000. And while C-3PO and&nbsp;
R2-D2 are always a hit with the fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.240
none of the side characters were really clicking.&nbsp;
Artist Brian Lemay later told SyFy Wire:

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.400
"The secondary characters that [the droids] were&nbsp;
involved with were not really strong characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.640
They were just these characters that were thrown&nbsp;
in to carry them through an adventure of sorts."

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.960
Worse still, this intensely family-friendly&nbsp;
show lacked any sort of edge. Future Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.080
Wars cartoons wouldn't be afraid to spice&nbsp;
things up a little, but Droids was allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.440
no such flourishes. The man behind C-3PO,&nbsp;
Anthony Daniels, even once complained:

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.040
"You can't put a custard pie in my face because&nbsp;
of what they call 'imitative behavior.' They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.960
don't want children saying, 'Oh, we saw it&nbsp;
on the cartoon, we thought it was okay.'"

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.680
Perhaps unsurprisingly, Droids didn't&nbsp;
last more than a single season.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.360
While The Clone Wars was on the air, plans got&nbsp;
underway for the show to spin off into another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.800
animated show. A series of Clone Wars episodes,&nbsp;
focusing on a collection of young Padawans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.320
aired during the series' fifth season in early&nbsp;
2012. This arc was supposed to establish a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.280
group of characters who would later star in&nbsp;
their own show. The episodes that introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.440
these figures centered on the younglings&nbsp;
trying to build their own lightsabers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.840
with the help of Ahsoka Tano and a&nbsp;
wise droid voiced by David Tennant.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.720
"So let us begin. There is&nbsp;
much more work to be done."

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.240
There was enough confidence in&nbsp;
this premise that the episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.280
were even planned to air as a single&nbsp;
TV movie before leading into a TV show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.040
mirroring the way in which Clone Wars&nbsp;
itself was introduced to the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.640
However, for reasons unknown, this storyline was&nbsp;
relegated to a string of traditional episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.120
while the spinoff was put on the backburner.&nbsp;
This was in spite of the new program being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.840
something of a passion project for George Lucas&nbsp;
himself. The production fell further into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.680
rearview once Disney bought Lucasfilm and set up&nbsp;
its own slate of kid-oriented Star Wars cartoons.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.440
With the creation of Xbox's Kinect, a&nbsp;
new world of video game possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.480
suddenly opened up. Gaming that relied on your&nbsp;
body movements rather than a controller was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.280
prospect that seemed so exciting at the time,&nbsp;
and it wasn't long before thoughts turned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.920
how the Star Wars universe could utilize&nbsp;
this idea. After all, Star Wars and video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.960
games have been a lucrative pairing for decades&nbsp;
now — so why not embrace this new technology?

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.400
Unfortunately, rather than using the Kinect&nbsp;
controllers to make a new action-adventure title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.240
LucasArts produced Kinect Star Wars, a grab bag&nbsp;
of different play modes, ranging from Padawan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:27.040
training to… dance-offs. Inevitably, Kinect Star&nbsp;
Wars failed to connect with the general public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.560
and critics were less than impressed. Some praise&nbsp;
was offered for certain aspects of the gameplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.760
but the project just didn't come&nbsp;
together like it was supposed to.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.440
As it stands, the only part of&nbsp;
Kinect Star Wars to have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.800
any lasting impact is one of its dance-off tunes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:49.440
titled "I'm Han Solo." This parody of "Ridin'&nbsp;
Solo" became infamous, with The A.V. Club calling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:56.080
it, quote, "one of the worst pieces of Star Wars&nbsp;
licensing ever." Not great, as far as legacies go.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.240
For a moment, it looked like The Force Unleashed&nbsp;
was the next big step for the Star Wars franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.520
The beloved 2008 video game followed the&nbsp;
tale of Starkiller, the hidden apprentice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.640
of Darth Vader. And it really seemed like this&nbsp;
premise might inspire all kinds of compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.840
storytelling for years to come. Sadly, much of&nbsp;
that potential was sucked away by its sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:24.080
The Force Unleashed II. This 2010 title received&nbsp;
mixed marks from critics, with much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.960
criticism being directed at the writing and&nbsp;
its awkward attempt at a cliffhanger ending.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.440
Still, many Star Wars properties have come&nbsp;
up short critically before going on to spawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.640
further sequels and spinoffs. The real kiss&nbsp;
of death for The Force Unleashed II was its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.840
sales numbers, which proved to be incredibly&nbsp;
underwhelming. With all these factors at play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.520
the continuing importance of The Force Unleashed&nbsp;
franchise began to diminish. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.720
this once-promising new avenue for Star&nbsp;
Wars storytelling sputtered out completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.520
as the Disney purchase of Lucasfilm led&nbsp;
to the prioritization of newer projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.600
Even without that merger, though,&nbsp;
it's doubtful that The Force Unleashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.000
II could ever have generated enough of a&nbsp;
positive response to keep the series going.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.640
Initially, the second Star Wars spinoff movie&nbsp;
after Rogue One wasn't going to be Han Solo's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.400
origin story. Instead, it was supposed to be a&nbsp;
Boba Fett feature. The controversial director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.480
Josh Trank was once set to helm the&nbsp;
production, but the project was put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.400
on the backburner once Trank stepped away. The&nbsp;
filmmaker opened up in May 2020, explaining:

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.880
"I quit because I knew I was going&nbsp;
to be fired if I didn't quit."

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:41.280
Still, this wasn't the end of the story for Fett's&nbsp;
solo movie. For a brief moment in May 2018, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.560
rumor that Logan director James Mangold stepped in&nbsp;
to take over from Trank, renewed public interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.920
in the project. But this interest was short-lived.&nbsp;
In the wake of Solo coming up short at the box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:58.400
office, spinoff Star Wars movies were put on hold&nbsp;
in favor of expanding the franchise through event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:04.240
TV shows on Disney+. This meant that the Boba&nbsp;
Fett movie was once again kicked to the curb.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.800
Eventually, Fett would make his way into&nbsp;
Disney's Star Wars galaxy in the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.960
season of The Mandalorian and his&nbsp;
own series, The Book of Boba Fett.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.880
"I'm a simple man making his way through&nbsp;
the galaxy, like my father before me.”

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.760
At the start of the 2010s, it looked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.800
theatrical 3D re-releases of classic&nbsp;
movies were about to become a big thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.960
With the original Lion King becoming a surprise&nbsp;
hit when it was released in 3D in September 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.960
the stage seemed set for a series of subsequent 3D&nbsp;
re-releases to make a similar killing at the box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:43.760
office. These included The Phantom Menace&nbsp;
3D, which was planned as the start of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:49.440
ambitious push to re-release all six original&nbsp;
Star Wars movies in a whole new dimension.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:55.040
Sadly, The Phantom Menace 3D didn't exactly&nbsp;
blow the doors off the box office. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:01.040
its worldwide gross was enough to push the movie's&nbsp;
lifetime haul past the $1 billion threshold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.920
but that was its only accomplishment. After&nbsp;
a decent opening weekend, the movie made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.520
just less than half of what The Lion King 3D&nbsp;
had accumulated at the domestic box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.880
Autumn 2013 release dates were set for&nbsp;
3D re-releases of the other two Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.800
prequel films, but they were eventually pulled&nbsp;
after Disney bought Lucasfilm. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.920
the studio's ambitious plans&nbsp;
have been left unrealized.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:32.560
Today, Star Wars: The Clone Wars is known as&nbsp;
a TV show that took major risks by focusing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.560
unsung supporting characters in the franchise, and&nbsp;
even exploring some outright esoteric concepts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.000
But you have to walk before you can run, and&nbsp;
most animated programs begin their runs with more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.120
awkward episodes. Rarely, though, do these&nbsp;
more imperfect episodes play out on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.320
big screen. But such was the case for the&nbsp;
theatrical film Star Wars: The Clone Wars.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:08:00.080
Released in 2008, The Clone Wars was meant to&nbsp;
serve as a feature-length pilot for the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.480
However, the film's animation style could&nbsp;
never hope to compete with the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:09.600
visuals seen in its big-budget competitors.&nbsp;
Worse, this movie utilized a more juvenile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.000
sense of humor and dialogue that later&nbsp;
seasons of The Clone Wars would jettison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.560
All in all, the whole project was quite poorly&nbsp;
thought-out, and it didn't even bring in much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:24.560
money for the studio, cementing the project as an&nbsp;
underwhelming beginning to a beloved TV series.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.080
George Lucas got along so well with the team&nbsp;
behind the trio of Robot Chicken Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.960
specials that he assembled the same group&nbsp;
in the late 2000s for a new sci-fi project.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.760
"As the creator of Mara Jade…"

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.360
"Wait?! I am not the creator of Mara Jade.”

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.640
“And I want you to get out&nbsp;
of this office right now!"

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:50.080
This was Star Wars: Detours, an animated&nbsp;
TV show that would take a satirical spin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.640
on many of the series' beloved characters.&nbsp;
Clips of the program were shown at Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.560
Wars Celebration in 2012, and included such&nbsp;
antics as Obi-Wan Kenobi becoming a comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:04.240
and Admiral Ackbar failing&nbsp;
to think of the word 'trap.'

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:07.520
Just two months after the world&nbsp;
discovered Detours, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:12.880
Disney bought Lucasfilm, and the whole landscape&nbsp;
of Star Wars entertainment changed forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.280
Disney had its own plans for the franchise,&nbsp;
of course, and none of them revolved around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.200
Detours. Even though around 40 episodes&nbsp;
of the show had been completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.920
the program was shelved. Seth Green later&nbsp;
confirmed that Lucasfilm's new management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.720
just didn't see the show fitting in with their&nbsp;
more serious-minded plans for the franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.240
Rumors continue to persist that Detours will&nbsp;
eventually drop on Disney+ in the near future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.960
but otherwise, this particular project&nbsp;
seems to have totally fizzled out.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.000
"Ted, I got your voucher."

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:42.640
[slapping]

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.140
"Out!"

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:48.400
“There”

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:49.840
"I'm sorry about that. I'm so sorry."

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:56.000
LEGO and Star Wars have turned out to be a match&nbsp;
made in heaven. The two pop culture properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:01.360
have been working together for decades now, on&nbsp;
everything from toys to video games to movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:07.120
So, why shouldn't there eventually be plans to&nbsp;
turn LEGO Star Wars into an ongoing TV show?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.440
Well, such a creative endeavor once&nbsp;
emerged through The Freemaker Adventures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.240
which followed a trio of siblings who get swept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.720
up in the greater battle between the&nbsp;
Rebel Alliance and the Galactic Empire.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:25.520
To date, this show is the only instance of a LEGO&nbsp;
Star Wars property scoring an ongoing TV program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:31.280
in a traditional seasonal format, with most other&nbsp;
televised LEGO Star Wars properties emerging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:36.800
as either TV specials or miniseries. And given&nbsp;
that The Freemaker Adventures didn't set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:42.160
world on fire, that's not surprising, either.&nbsp;
The show wasn't exactly a massive flop, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:47.200
was nowhere near as impactful as Lucasfilm's other&nbsp;
animated shows, and it didn't attract the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:53.280
fanbase that the LEGO Star Wars video games have.&nbsp;
In the end, the show lasted only two seasons.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:58.800
Originally, the next step for the Star Wars&nbsp;
franchise after Revenge of the Sith wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:04.640
a sequel trilogy, but a foray into the world of&nbsp;
television. The Clone Wars was to begin airing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:11.520
2008, and then a live-action Star Wars TV show,&nbsp;
later named Star Wars: Underworld, would debut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:17.040
shortly thereafter. The program was set to explore&nbsp;
all kinds of nooks and crannies of the galaxy far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:23.200
far away. It would cover everything from how Han&nbsp;
and Chewie met to the origin story of Palpatine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:28.560
The series had grand ambitions indeed, and&nbsp;
was personally overseen by George Lucas.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:34.480
But Underworld never got off the ground, for&nbsp;
one simple reason: money. The financial strain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.160
required to pull off a production of this scale&nbsp;
on a network TV budget proved too massive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:46.960
overcome. Lucasfilm executive Pablo Hidalgo later&nbsp;
noted that George Lucas was unable to expand his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:52.880
Grady Ranch property to accommodate soundstages,&nbsp;
which also forced the show's production to ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:58.720
to a halt. Nowadays, shows such as The Mandalorian&nbsp;
have made Star Wars television practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:04.880
commonplace. In that sense, perhaps Star Wars:&nbsp;
Underworld was simply a project ahead of its time.

